{
  "statement": "Whether AI reshapes jobs, privacy, and public safety in the next five years or the next fifty depends partly on whether oversight systems exist when the technology arrives. The Federal Aviation Administration spent decades building its certification framework. That framework still failed when Boeing self-certified the 737 MAX. 346 people died because oversight was delegated to the manufacturer. The FDA's drug approval pipeline requires 7 to 10 years of phased review before a new medicine reaches patients. These are systems built over generations against well-understood risks. AI oversight must be built against a moving target with no settled risk categories, no agreed evaluation metrics, and no international coordination mechanism. That infrastructure does not yet exist in operational form anywhere. Regulatory lead times are measured in decades. Even conservative AI timeline forecasts cluster inside that window.\n\nThe most credible forecast is not any expert survey. It is the capital allocation of the companies building these systems. Major AI labs have committed tens of billions of dollars to physical compute infrastructure. OpenAI's Sam Altman, Google DeepMind's Demis Hassabis, and Anthropic's Dario Amodei have each publicly estimated human-level AI within 3 to 10 years. When actors risk billions of dollars on a timeline assumption, that revealed preference carries more evidential weight than any academic prediction market. Regulators are not being asked to out-predict forecasters. They are being asked to match the pace that developers themselves have already set.\n\nTimeline uncertainty does not prevent effective regulation. It changes what kind of regulation to build. Compute tracking already has precedent in US export controls restricting advanced chip sales to certain regions. Pre-deployment impact assessments have precedent in environmental impact reports and pharmaceutical clinical trials. Liability frameworks for high-consequence systems have precedent in nuclear regulation and aviation manufacturer liability. None of these mechanisms require knowing when advanced AI arrives. They require knowing that capability is scaling, which is observable today. The question is not whether oversight is possible under uncertainty. It is whether the infrastructure will be built before it is needed.\n\nThe cost of delay is not measured in missed opportunities. It is measured in the distance between what a system can do and what anyone can verify, halt, or override. Every month that passes without oversight infrastructure widens that distance. The Boeing 737 MAX crashes were preventable. The warning signs were visible years before the first crash. What was missing was not knowledge of the risk. It was the institutional willingness to act before harm became undeniable. Watch for whether governments require pre-deployment safety assessments for large AI systems. Watch for whether compute thresholds trigger mandatory review. If neither exists when capabilities arrive, the gap between what AI can do and what anyone can control will be the only legacy of this debate.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🏗️", "tooltip": "Regulatory infrastructure takes decades to build, and AI developers are already spending billions as if that window is closing."},
    {"symbol": "🚧", "tooltip": "Guardrails must exist before the road is traveled, not reconstructed after the crash."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Regulatory lead times are measured in decades. Even conservative AI timeline forecasts cluster inside that window.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "When actors risk billions of dollars on a timeline assumption, that revealed preference carries more evidential weight than any academic prediction market.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "None of these mechanisms require knowing when advanced AI arrives. They require knowing that capability is scaling, which is observable today.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}
